{"ml_models": "ML models, or machine learning models, are algorithms that learn patterns from data to make predictions or decisions. They can improve over time with more data and are used across various fields, including medicine and post-transplant care, to enhance diagnostic processes, predict outcomes, and manage treatments."},
{"ml_models__have_been_analyzed__using_ml_models": "The phrase \"have been analyzed\" indicates that a process of examination and evaluation has been conducted on the subject. In this case, it suggests that ML models have undergone scrutiny to understand their effectiveness or characteristics."},
{"using_ml_models": "\"Using ML models\" as an object in this context implies that the analysis itself was conducted with the aid of machine learning models. This suggests a recursive or meta approach where ML models are used to analyze other ML models, possibly to evaluate their performance or to understand their underlying mechanisms."},
{"this_term": "This term refers to a specific terminology or phrase that has been chosen for use in the manuscript. It is likely a key concept or method that the authors have decided to highlight or emphasize in their work."},
{"this_term__is_preferred__throughout_the_manuscript": "The predicate \"is preferred\" indicates a deliberate choice or preference for using a particular term or phrase over others. This suggests that the authors have evaluated various options and have selected this term as the most appropriate or effective for their purposes."},
{"throughout_the_manuscript": "Throughout the manuscript signifies that the chosen term is consistently used across the entire document. This ensures uniformity and clarity in the communication of ideas, making it easier for readers to follow and understand the authors' arguments or findings."},
{"classical_statistical_approach": "The classical statistical approach refers to traditional methods used in statistical analysis, which have been widely adopted and standardized since the 1940s. It involves a set of techniques that rely on making assumptions about the distribution of data to derive results."},
{"classical_statistical_approach__is_based_on__inferential_analyses": "The phrase \"is based on\" indicates that the classical statistical approach relies fundamentally on a specific type of analysis. This relationship suggests that the approach cannot function without the underlying principles or methods it is founded upon."},
{"inferential_analyses": "Inferential analyses are a type of statistical analysis that involves making inferences or predictions about a population based on a sample of data taken from the population. This method is crucial in the classical statistical approach as it allows for the generalization of results beyond the observed data."},
{"results": "The results refer to the outcomes or findings obtained from the classical statistical approach based on inferential analyses."},
{"results__are_derived__making_strong_assumptions_about_the_data_distribution": "The phrase 'are derived' indicates the process by which the results are obtained or concluded from the inferential analyses."},
{"making_strong_assumptions_about_the_data_distribution": "Making strong assumptions about the data distribution refers to the requirement in classical statistical methods to assume a specific form or pattern in the data, which is crucial for the validity of the inferential analyses."},
{"inferential_model": "The inferential model is a classical statistical approach that involves deriving results from data by making strong assumptions about the data distribution. It is commonly used in scientific analyses to make inferences based on observed data."},
{"inferential_model__has_been__de_facto_the_standard_procedure_for_analyzing_scientific_experiments_since_1940": "The phrase \"has been\" indicates a historical and ongoing status of the inferential model as a standard method. It suggests that the model has maintained its position as a primary tool for analysis over a significant period."},
{"de_facto_the_standard_procedure_for_analyzing_scientific_experiments_since_1940": "Being \"de facto the standard procedure for analyzing scientific experiments since 1940\" means that the inferential model has been widely accepted and used as the default method for analyzing scientific data for many decades, starting from the year 1940."},
