{"particular_conditions__include__refractory_ascites_or_hepatocellular_carcinoma": "The predicate 'include' signifies that the subject encompasses or consists of the specified conditions, highlighting their relevance and importance in the context of the discussion."},
{"refractory_ascites_or_hepatocellular_carcinoma": "Refractory ascites is a severe form of fluid accumulation in the abdomen that does not respond to treatment, while hepatocellular carcinoma is a type of liver cancer. Both conditions are common among cirrhotic patients and significantly impact their prognosis and need for a liver transplant."},
{"ml__has_shown_promise_in__making_the_short_term_prognosis_for_patients_awaiting_a_transplant_more_reliable": "\"Has shown promise in\" indicates that ML has demonstrated potential effectiveness and positive results in a specific application. In this case, it suggests that ML techniques have been successfully applied to enhance the accuracy and reliability of short-term prognosis for patients awaiting a transplant."},
{"making_the_short_term_prognosis_for_patients_awaiting_a_transplant_more_reliable": "Making the short-term prognosis for patients awaiting a transplant more reliable involves improving the accuracy of predictions about patient outcomes within a short timeframe, typically a few months. This is crucial for optimizing organ allocation and ensuring that the most critical patients receive transplants in a timely manner, ultimately reducing waiting list mortality."},
{"bertsimas_et_al": "Bertsimas et al. refers to a group of researchers led by Dimitris Bertsimas, who are recognized for their work in developing advanced models for various applications, including healthcare and organ allocation."},
{"bertsimas_et_al__developed__a_model_using_ml_with_optimal_classification_trees": "The term \"developed\" indicates that Bertsimas et al. created and formulated a new model. This process involved designing, testing, and refining the model to ensure its effectiveness and reliability in predicting outcomes."},
{"a_model_using_ml_with_optimal_classification_trees": "The model using ML with optimal classification trees is an advanced machine learning-based system designed by Bertsimas et al. to predict mortality or waiting list removal for cirrhotic patients listed for liver transplantation. This model aims to provide more accurate short-term prognosis compared to traditional scoring systems like MELD-Na."},
{"model": "The model refers to a machine learning-based system developed by Bertsimas et al. using optimal classification trees. It was created to improve the prediction of outcomes for cirrhotic patients awaiting liver transplantation."},
{"model__predicts__mortality_or_waiting_list_removal_in_cirrhotic_patients_listed_for_lt": "The predicate 'predicts' indicates the model's capability to forecast future events or outcomes based on the input data it processes. In this context, it specifically refers to the model's ability to estimate the likelihood of certain events happening to patients on the liver transplant waiting list."},
{"mortality_or_waiting_list_removal_in_cirrhotic_patients_listed_for_lt": "Mortality or waiting list removal in cirrhotic patients listed for LT refers to the critical outcomes the model is designed to predict. These outcomes include the death of patients while they are on the waiting list or their removal from the list for reasons other than receiving a transplant, which are significant concerns in the management of liver transplant candidates."},
{"data": "The data refers to information collected and analyzed for the purpose of developing a predictive model for mortality or waiting list removal in cirrhotic patients listed for liver transplantation."},
{"data__is_from__waitlisted_patients_between_2002_and_2016_in_the_usa": "The phrase \"is from\" indicates the source or origin of the data, specifying the particular group and time period from which the data was gathered."},
{"waitlisted_patients_between_2002_and_2016_in_the_usa": "Waitlisted patients between 2002 and 2016 in the USA represent the specific population from which the data was derived. This group includes individuals who were on the waiting list for a liver transplant during the specified time frame within the United States."},
{"optimized_prediction_of_mortality_score": "The optimized prediction of mortality score is a model developed using machine learning with optimal classification trees to predict mortality or waiting list removal in cirrhotic patients listed for liver transplantation. It was created using data from waitlisted patients between 2002 and 2016 in the USA.|Here is another description: The optimized prediction of mortality score is a model developed using machine learning with optimal classification trees to predict mortality or waiting list removal in cirrhotic patients listed for liver transplantation. It was created using data from waitlisted patients between 2002 and 2016 in the USA."},
{"optimized_prediction_of_mortality_score__was_run_in__a_liver_simulated_allocation_model": "The phrase \"was run in\" indicates that the optimized prediction of mortality score was tested or applied within the context of a liver simulated allocation model to evaluate its effectiveness and accuracy in predicting patient outcomes."},
{"a_liver_simulated_allocation_model": "A liver simulated allocation model is a tool used to simulate the allocation of livers for transplantation. It helps in assessing the effectiveness of different scoring systems, like the optimized prediction of mortality score, in predicting patient outcomes and improving the allocation process."},
{"optimized_prediction_of_mortality_score__predicts_3_month_mortality_better_than__meld_na_scores_auc_0_859_vs_0_841_or_matchmeld_scores_auc_0_859_vs_0_823": "The predicate \"predicts 3-month mortality better than\" indicates that the optimized prediction of mortality score has a higher accuracy in forecasting the likelihood of death within three months compared to other scoring systems. This is measured using the Area Under the Curve (AUC) metric, where a higher AUC value signifies better predictive performance."},
{"meld_na_scores_auc_0_859_vs_0_841_or_matchmeld_scores_auc_0_859_vs_0_823": "The object entities are the MELD-Na scores and MatchMELD scores, which are existing scoring systems used to predict mortality in patients awaiting liver transplantation. The MELD-Na score has an AUC of 0.841, while the MatchMELD score has an AUC of 0.823, both of which are lower than the AUC of 0.859 achieved by the optimized prediction of mortality score."},
{"prediction_accuracy": "Prediction accuracy refers to the effectiveness of a model or scoring system in correctly forecasting outcomes, such as mortality or waiting list removal, for patients awaiting liver transplants."},
{"prediction_accuracy__differed_between__the_new_model_and_meld_na_especially_among_the_sickest_candidates": "Differed between indicates a comparison where the effectiveness or results of one model or scoring system are not the same as another, highlighting variations in performance under specific conditions."},
{"the_new_model_and_meld_na_especially_among_the_sickest_candidates": "The new model and MELD-Na especially among the sickest candidates refers to a machine learning-based model developed by Bertsimas et al. for predicting mortality in liver transplant candidates, compared to the MELD-Na scoring system, with a particular focus on how they perform among the most critically ill patients."},
{"new_model": "The new model refers to an optimized prediction of mortality score developed using machine learning with optimal classification trees. This model was created to improve the prediction of 3-month mortality for cirrhotic patients listed for liver transplantation."},
{"new_model__saves_on_average_at_least__418_more_lives_per_year_when_compared_with_the_currently_adopted_meld_based_system": "The phrase \"saves on average at least\" indicates a quantifiable benefit provided by the new model in terms of lives saved per year. This benefit is measured by comparing the new model's performance to that of the currently adopted MELD-based system."},
{"418_more_lives_per_year_when_compared_with_the_currently_adopted_meld_based_system": "The object \"418 more lives per year\" represents the minimum average number of additional lives saved annually by the new model when compared to the MELD-based system. This metric highlights the significant improvement in patient outcomes achieved by the new model."},
{"cucchetti_et_al": "Cucchetti et al. is a group of researchers who conducted a study involving the construction of an artificial neural network (ANN) to predict 3-month outcomes for patients awaiting liver transplants.|Here is another description: Cucchetti et al. is a research team or group of authors who have conducted studies in the field of medical prediction models, specifically focusing on liver transplant outcomes."},
{"cucchetti_et_al__examined__this_topic": "The predicate 'examined' indicates that Cucchetti et al. investigated or studied a particular topic using their ANN model, focusing on its predictive capabilities compared to the MELD score."},
{"this_topic": "The object 'this topic' refers to the specific area of research Cucchetti et al. focused on, which involves predicting 3-month outcomes for liver transplant patients using an ANN, and comparing its performance to the MELD score."},
{"cucchetti_et_al__constructed__an_artificial_neural_network_ann": "The predicate 'constructed' indicates that Cucchetti et al. built or developed something, in this case, a predictive model, through a process that likely involved designing, programming, and testing."},
{"an_artificial_neural_network_ann": "An artificial neural network (ANN) is a computational model inspired by the human brain's structure and function. It consists of layers of interconnected nodes or 'neurons' that process information. In this context, the ANN was specifically designed to predict 3-month outcomes for patients awaiting liver transplants, using biochemical parameters recorded at the time of waiting list registration."},
{"ann": "ANN, or Artificial Neural Network, is a computational model inspired by the structure and function of the human brain. It is designed to simulate the way biological neurons process information, allowing it to learn from data and make predictions or decisions.|Here is another description: The ANN, or artificial neural network, is a brain-inspired model designed to mimic the structure and function of biological neurons. It was constructed by Cucchetti et al. to predict 3-month outcomes for patients awaiting liver transplants, utilizing ten biochemical parameters commonly recorded at the time of waiting list registration."},
{"ann__resembles__biological_neurons": "The predicate'resembles' indicates a similarity or likeness between the subject (ANN) and the object (biological neurons). In this context, it suggests that the ANN is designed to mimic or model the behavior and functionality of biological neurons."},
{"biological_neurons": "Biological neurons are the fundamental units of the brain and nervous system. They are specialized cells designed to transmit information to other nerve cells, muscle, or gland cells. Neurons have distinct structures, including dendrites, a cell body, and an axon, which facilitate the transmission of electrical and chemical signals."},
{"network": "The subject, 'network', refers to an artificial neural network (ANN) constructed by Cucchetti et al. This ANN is a computational model inspired by the structure and function of biological neurons, designed to mimic the way the human brain processes information."},
{"network__adjusts__the_weights": "The predicate 'adjusts' describes the process by which the ANN modifies its internal parameters during the learning phase to improve its predictive capabilities. This adjustment is a fundamental aspect of machine learning, allowing the model to learn from data and make more accurate predictions over time."},
{"the_weights": "The object 'the weights' refers to the internal parameters of the ANN that are adjusted during the learning process. These weights represent the strengths of the connections (synapses) between the virtual neurons within the network, and their optimization is crucial for enhancing the model's predictive performance."},
{"weights": "Weights in the context of an artificial neural network (ANN) refer to the adjustable parameters that represent the strengths of the connections (synapses) between virtual neurons. These weights are crucial for the network's operation and are modified during the learning phase to optimize the network's performance."},
{"weights__increase__predictive_ability": "The predicate \"increase\" in this context signifies the enhancement or improvement of a certain capability or performance metric. Specifically, it denotes the process by which the ANN's predictive ability is augmented through the adjustment of weights during the learning phase."},
{"predictive_ability": "Predictive ability in this context refers to the ANN's capacity to accurately forecast outcomes, such as 3-month mortality in patients awaiting a transplant. This ability is quantified and compared against other metrics, like the MELD score, to evaluate the effectiveness of the ANN model."},
{"ann_model": "The ANN model is a machine learning model that uses artificial neural networks to predict post-transplant outcomes in liver transplantation. Developed by researchers such as Cucchetti and Briceno, it adjusts weights during the learning phase to enhance predictive accuracy. The model considers a combination of 57 variables related to donors, recipients, and surgery to improve predictions over traditional scoring systems.|Here is another description: The ANN model is a type of machine learning model that was applied to predict acute rejection early after liver transplantation in adult recipients. It utilized biochemical characteristics and timing since transplantation to make its predictions.|Here is another description: The ANN model is a type of machine learning model that was developed by Marsh et al. to predict hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) recurrence at 1, 2, and 3 years post-liver transplant (LT). It is based on five risk factors including sex, tumor number, size and intrahepatic distribution, and grade of vascular invasion."},
{"ann_model__designed_to_predict__3_month_outcome": "The predicate \"designed to predict\" indicates the purpose for which the ANN model was created. It signifies that the model was specifically configured and trained with the intention of forecasting a particular outcome based on input data."},
{"3_month_outcome": "The 3-month outcome refers to the predicted health status or mortality of patients within a three-month period following a specific event, such as being placed on a transplant waiting list. This outcome was the target of the ANN model's predictions in the study by Cucchetti et al."},
{"ann_model__included__ten_biochemical_parameters": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the ANN model was constructed with certain elements as part of its structure or input data. In this context, it indicates that the model was built to incorporate specific parameters for its predictions."},
{"ten_biochemical_parameters": "The ten biochemical parameters are specific measurements commonly recorded at the time of waiting list registration for liver transplant patients. These parameters are part of the input data used by the ANN model to make predictions about patient outcomes."},
{"cohorts": "The subject refers to two groups of patients, one Italian and one English, who were part of a study examining the predictive ability of an artificial neural network (ANN) for 3-month outcomes in liver transplant candidates. These cohorts were used for internal and external validation of the ANN model."},
{"cohorts__differed_slightly_regarding__indications_to_lt_or_severity_of_underlying_liver_disease": "The predicate indicates a minor variation or discrepancy between the two patient cohorts in specific aspects related to their medical conditions and treatment. It suggests that while the cohorts were similar in many ways, there were slight differences that needed to be acknowledged in the study."},
{"indications_to_lt_or_severity_of_underlying_liver_disease": "The object describes the specific areas where the cohorts differed: the indications for liver transplantation (LT) and the severity of the underlying liver disease. These differences are quantified by the mean Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) scores, which were 16.7 for the Italian cohort and 14.7 for the English patients."},
{"mean_meld_scores": "The mean MELD scores represent the average Model for End-Stage Liver Disease scores calculated for two distinct patient cohorts, one from Italy and the other from England, who were awaiting liver transplants."},
{"mean_meld_scores__were__16_7_for_the_italian_cohort_and_14_7_for_the_english_patients": "The predicate 'were' indicates the existence or occurrence of the mean MELD scores at a specific point in time for the two patient cohorts, reflecting their respective levels of liver disease severity."},
{"16_7_for_the_italian_cohort_and_14_7_for_the_english_patients": "The objects '16.7 for the Italian cohort and 14.7 for the English patients' specify the exact mean MELD scores for each cohort, with the Italian cohort having a higher average score, indicating potentially more severe liver disease compared to the English patients."},
{"ann__performed_better_than__the_meld_score_in_predicting_3_months_mortality": "The phrase \"performed better than\" indicates a comparative evaluation where the ANN's predictive performance was superior to that of the MELD score. This comparison is based on the area under the curve (AUC) values, which measure the accuracy of the predictions."},
{"the_meld_score_in_predicting_3_months_mortality": "The MELD score is a numerical scale used to assess the severity of liver disease and predict mortality risk in patients with end-stage liver disease. In the context provided, it serves as a benchmark against which the ANN's predictive performance is compared for predicting 3-month mortality in patients awaiting liver transplants."},
{"ann_vs_meld_auc": "The ANN vs. MELD AUC refers to the comparison of the area under the curve (AUC) values between an artificial neural network (ANN) model and the Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD) score, used to predict outcomes in liver disease patients."},
{"ann_vs_meld_auc__has_value__0_94_0_99": "The predicate \"has value\" indicates that the ANN vs. MELD AUC comparison yields specific numerical results, reflecting the performance of the ANN model relative to the MELD score in predicting patient outcomes."},
{"0_94_0_99": "The object \"0.94\u20130.99\" represents the range of AUC values obtained from the ANN model in comparison to the MELD score, indicating a high predictive accuracy of the ANN model within this range."},
{"meld_auc": "MELD AUC refers to the Area Under the Curve (AUC) of the Model for End-Stage Liver Disease (MELD), a scoring system used to assess the severity of liver disease and predict mortality."},
{"meld_auc__has_value__0_86_0_80_0_91": "The predicate \"has value\" indicates that the MELD AUC is quantified with a specific numerical value, reflecting its performance or accuracy in a given context."},
{"0_86_0_80_0_91": "The value 0.86 [0.80\u20130.91] represents the MELD AUC with a 95% confidence interval, indicating the range within which the true AUC is likely to fall, showcasing the model's predictive accuracy."},
{"p_value": "The P value is a statistical measure used to determine the significance of results in a hypothesis test. In this context, it is used to compare the performance of two models, ANN and MELD, in predicting outcomes.|Here is another description: The P value is a statistical measure used to determine the significance of results in a test, indicating the probability that the observed data could have occurred under the null hypothesis."},
{"p_value__is__0_002": "The predicate \"is\" indicates the specific value of the P value in the statistical comparison between the ANN and MELD models. It signifies that the P value has been calculated and found to be 0.002."},
{"0_002": "The object \"0.002\" is the calculated P value from the statistical test comparing the ANN and MELD models. This value indicates a very low probability that the observed difference in performance between the two models occurred by random chance, suggesting a statistically significant difference."},
{"internal_validation_cohort_ann_vs_meld_auc": "The internal validation cohort refers to a specific group of data used to assess the performance of a model internally, within the same dataset from which the model was derived. This cohort is crucial for evaluating the model's reliability and accuracy before it is tested on external datasets."},
{"internal_validation_cohort_ann_vs_meld_auc__has_value__0_95_0_86_0_99": "The predicate \"has value\" indicates the specific measurement or result obtained from the internal validation cohort when comparing the ANN (Artificial Neural Network) model's performance to the MELD (Model for End-stage Liver Disease) score. This comparison is quantified through the Area Under the Curve (AUC), a common metric for evaluating diagnostic tests."},
{"0_95_0_86_0_99": "The object \"0.95 [0.86\u20130.99]\" represents the AUC value with a 95% confidence interval for the ANN model in the internal validation cohort. An AUC of 0.95 suggests excellent discriminatory ability of the ANN model, with the confidence interval providing a range within which the true AUC is likely to fall, indicating the precision of the estimate."},
{"internal_validation_cohort_meld_auc": "The internal validation cohort refers to a specific group of data used to test and validate the performance of a model or method within the same dataset from which it was derived. This cohort is crucial for assessing the reliability and accuracy of the model before it is applied to external datasets."},
{"internal_validation_cohort_meld_auc__has_value__0_85_0_74_0_96": "The predicate \"has value\" indicates that a specific metric or measurement has been calculated and assigned to the subject. In this context, it signifies that the MELD AUC (Model for End-Stage Liver Disease Area Under the Curve) has been quantified for the internal validation cohort."},
{"0_85_0_74_0_96": "The object \"0.85 [0.74\u20130.96]\" represents the calculated value of the MELD AUC for the internal validation cohort, along with its 95% confidence interval. This value indicates the performance of the MELD model in predicting outcomes within this specific cohort, with the confidence interval providing a range within which the true value is likely to fall."},
{"p_value__is__0_032": "The predicate \"is\" in this context signifies the assignment of a specific numerical value to the P value, reflecting the outcome of a statistical test comparing two different methods or models."},
{"0_032": "The object \"0.032\" is the specific P value obtained from the statistical comparison between the ANN (Artificial Neural Network) and MELD (Model for End-Stage Liver Disease) AUC (Area Under the Curve) in an external cohort, suggesting a statistically significant difference at this level."},
{"external_cohort_ann_vs_meld_auc": "The external cohort refers to a group of patients or data set that is used to validate the performance of a model or scoring system, distinct from the internal validation cohort. It is crucial for assessing the generalizability of the model's predictions."},
{"external_cohort_ann_vs_meld_auc__has_value__0_96": "The predicate \"has value\" indicates that a specific metric or score has been calculated and assigned to the external cohort. In this context, it refers to the Area Under the Curve (AUC) values obtained from comparing two different models or scoring systems."},
{"0_96": "The object \"0.96\" represents the AUC value for the Artificial Neural Network (ANN) model when applied to the external cohort. This value signifies the model's ability to distinguish between different outcomes, with higher values indicating better discriminatory performance."},
{"0_91_0_98": "The range 0.91\u20130.98 represents a specific set of values that are being compared in a statistical context, likely indicating a higher or more favorable range in a given measurement or metric."},
{"0_91_0_98__vs__0_86_0_79_0_91": "The predicate \"vs.\" denotes a comparison between two sets of values, suggesting that the subject range is being evaluated against another range to determine differences or significance."},
