{"long_term_outcome": "The long-term outcome refers to the health status or medical condition of patients more than one year after undergoing liver transplantation (LT), which is a critical measure of the success and efficacy of the transplantation process and post-operative care.|Here is another description: The long-term outcome refers to the health status and survival rates of patients who have undergone a liver transplant (LT) over an extended period. It is a critical measure used to evaluate the success and effectiveness of the transplant procedure and the overall management of the patient post-surgery."},
{"long_term_outcome__beyond__one_year_after_lt": "The term \"beyond\" in this context signifies a time frame that extends past a specific point, which in this case is one year after the liver transplant. It indicates that the assessment of outcomes is not limited to the immediate post-transplant period but includes a longer duration to capture the full spectrum of patient recovery and health progression."},
{"one_year_after_lt": "One year after LT refers to the period starting from the first anniversary of the liver transplant surgery. This time point is significant as it often marks the transition from the acute post-operative phase to a more stable phase where long-term health outcomes can be more accurately assessed."},
