{"cox_s_proportional_hazards_model": "Cox\u2019s proportional hazards model is a statistical technique used to analyze the effect of several variables on the time a specified event takes to happen. It is commonly used in survival analysis to estimate the impact of different factors on patient survival times."},
{"cox_s_proportional_hazards_model__retrieved__auc_standard_error_0_806_0_067": "The predicate \"retrieved\" in this context refers to the process of obtaining or calculating a specific metric or value using the Cox\u2019s proportional hazards model. Here, it indicates that the model was used to derive the AUC (Area Under the Curve) value along with its standard error."},
{"auc_standard_error_0_806_0_067": "AUC (Area Under the Curve) with a standard error of 0.806 (0.067) represents a measure of the model's accuracy in predicting patient outcomes. An AUC of 0.806 suggests a good discriminatory ability of the model, while the standard error of 0.067 provides an estimate of the precision of this measurement."},
