{"ann_model_to_predict_acute_rejection_early_after_lt": "The ANN model to predict acute rejection early after LT refers to an artificial neural network model developed and used by Hughes et al. to forecast the occurrence of acute rejection in liver transplant recipients at an early stage post-transplantation, based on various biochemical and timing-related factors."},
{"ann_model__showed__good_accuracy_auc_95_ci_0_902_0_861_0_944": "The predicate \"showed\" indicates that the ANN model demonstrated or exhibited a certain performance characteristic when applied to the task of predicting acute rejection after liver transplantation."},
{"good_accuracy_auc_95_ci_0_902_0_861_0_944": "Good accuracy, as measured by an AUC (Area Under the Curve) of 0.902 with a 95% confidence interval of 0.861 to 0.944, represents the performance of the ANN model in predicting acute rejection. This suggests that the model has a high level of discriminatory ability in its predictions."},
{"hepatocellular_carcinoma_hcc_recurrence": "Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) recurrence refers to the return of liver cancer after a liver transplant (LT), which is a critical concern in post-transplant care."},
{"hepatocellular_carcinoma_hcc_recurrence__has_been_demonstrated__in_6_18_of_patients_after_lt": "The phrase \"has been demonstrated\" indicates that the occurrence of HCC recurrence has been proven or shown to exist in a specific percentage of patients following a liver transplant."},
{"in_6_18_of_patients_after_lt": "The object \"in 6\u201318% of patients after LT\" specifies the range of patients who experience HCC recurrence post-transplant, highlighting the variability and significance of this complication in the context of liver transplantation."},
{"composite_models_of_hcc_recurrence": "Composite models of HCC recurrence are advanced predictive frameworks that integrate various morphological, clinical, and biochemical characteristics to assess the likelihood of hepatocellular carcinoma returning after liver transplantation."},
{"composite_models_of_hcc_recurrence__have_been_proposed__with_a_view_to_optimizing_post_lt_surveillance_stratifying_patients_risk_and_tailoring_their_immunosuppressant_therapy": "The phrase \"have been proposed\" indicates that these composite models were suggested and developed as a solution to improve post-transplant outcomes for patients with HCC."},
{"with_a_view_to_optimizing_post_lt_surveillance_stratifying_patients_risk_and_tailoring_their_immunosuppressant_therapy": "The goal of optimizing post-LT surveillance, stratifying patients\u2019 risk, and tailoring their immunosuppressant therapy involves using these models to enhance monitoring after liver transplantation, categorize patients based on their risk levels, and adjust their treatment plans accordingly to prevent HCC recurrence."},
{"marsh_et_al": "Marsh et al. refers to a research group led by an individual named Marsh, who conducted a study involving the development of an artificial neural network (ANN) model. This group is recognized for their work in applying machine learning techniques to medical prognosis, specifically in the context of liver transplantation outcomes."},
{"marsh_et_al__developed__ann_model_for_predicting_hcc_recurrence_at_1_2_and_3_years_post_lt": "The predicate \"developed\" indicates that Marsh et al. created or formulated the ANN model. This action involves designing the architecture of the model, selecting appropriate algorithms, and training the model using relevant data to achieve the desired predictive capabilities."},
{"ann_model_for_predicting_hcc_recurrence_at_1_2_and_3_years_post_lt": "The ANN model for predicting HCC recurrence at 1, 2, and 3 years post-LT is a machine learning tool designed by Marsh et al. to forecast the likelihood of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) returning after a liver transplant (LT). The model is based on several risk factors and is intended to help stratify patients according to their risk of HCC recurrence, thereby aiding in the customization of postoperative surveillance and treatment plans."},
{"ann_model__allowed__to_stratify_patients_into_three_groups_with_different_risk_of_recurrence": "The predicate \"allowed\" in this context refers to the capability of the ANN model to enable or facilitate a specific function or outcome. In this case, it allowed for the stratification of patients into different risk groups based on their likelihood of HCC recurrence post-LT."},
{"to_stratify_patients_into_three_groups_with_different_risk_of_recurrence": "The object \"to stratify patients into three groups with different risk of recurrence\" refers to the outcome or result that the ANN model facilitated. This stratification is crucial for optimizing post-LT surveillance and tailoring patients' immunosuppressant therapy according to their individual risk levels."},
{"the_same_group": "The same group refers to the research team led by Marsh et al., who previously developed an artificial neural network (ANN) model for predicting hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) recurrence after liver transplantation (LT)."},
{"the_same_group__adopted__the_previously_developed_ann_model_in_combination_with_tissue_genotyping_for_microsatellite_mutations_or_deletions_in_103_explanted_livers_with_hcc": "The predicate 'adopted' signifies that the research group implemented or utilized the previously developed ANN model in their study, integrating it with additional methods for enhanced prediction accuracy."},
{"the_previously_developed_ann_model_in_combination_with_tissue_genotyping_for_microsatellite_mutations_or_deletions_in_103_explanted_livers_with_hcc": "The object describes the specific application of the ANN model combined with tissue genotyping for microsatellite mutations or deletions in 103 explanted livers with HCC. This approach aimed to improve the predictive accuracy of post-LT HCC recurrence."},
{"combination": "The subject, \"Combination,\" refers to the integration of an artificial neural network (ANN) model with tissue genotyping for microsatellite mutations or deletions in explanted livers with hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)."},
{"combination__increased__predictive_accuracy_of_ann_regarding_post_lt_hcc_recurrence_by_15_88_vs_71": "The predicate, \"increased,\" indicates that the combination of the ANN model and tissue genotyping led to a higher level of predictive accuracy for post-liver transplant (LT) HCC recurrence."},
{"predictive_accuracy_of_ann_regarding_post_lt_hcc_recurrence_by_15_88_vs_71": "The object, \"predictive accuracy of ANN regarding post-LT HCC recurrence by 15% (88% vs. 71%),\" describes the enhanced ability of the combined model to predict HCC recurrence after liver transplantation, with an increase in accuracy from 71% to 88%."},
{"standard_statistical_logistic_regression_model": "The standard statistical logistic regression model is a traditional method used for predictive analysis, particularly in medical research, to estimate the probability of a binary outcome based on one or more predictor variables."},
{"standard_statistical_logistic_regression_model__vs__random_forest": "The comparison \"vs.\" indicates a contrast or evaluation between the standard statistical logistic regression model and another method, in this case, the random forest model, to assess their effectiveness in predictive accuracy and other performance metrics."},
{"auc_95_ci": "AUC (95% CI) refers to the Area Under the Curve with a 95% Confidence Interval, a statistical measure used to evaluate the performance of a diagnostic test or model, indicating its ability to distinguish between different classes or outcomes."},
{"auc_95_ci__is__0_61_0_56_0_66_vs_0_85_0_81_0_89": "The predicate \"is\" signifies the direct comparison or measurement of the AUC (95% CI) values between two different models or methods, in this case, a random forest model versus a logistic regression model."},
{"0_61_0_56_0_66_vs_0_85_0_81_0_89": "The object \"0.61 (0.56\u20130.66) vs. 0.85 (0.81\u20130.89)\" represents the specific AUC (95% CI) values obtained from the random forest model and the logistic regression model, respectively, highlighting a significant difference in their predictive performances."},
