{"ml_based_models__have_been_used_to__predict_survival_in_noncirrhotic_patients_awaiting_a_transplant": "The phrase \"have been used to\" indicates the application or utilization of ML-based models in a specific context or for a particular purpose. In this case, it highlights the role of these models in making predictions related to patient outcomes."},
{"predict_survival_in_noncirrhotic_patients_awaiting_a_transplant": "Predicting survival in noncirrhotic patients awaiting a transplant involves using data and algorithms to forecast the likelihood of survival for patients who do not have cirrhosis but are waiting for a transplant. This is a critical area of research as it can help in better patient management and resource allocation."},
{"speiser_et_al": "Speiser et al. refers to the authors of a proof-of-concept study that utilized machine learning models to predict patient outcomes. Their research focused on noncirrhotic patients awaiting a transplant, specifically those with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure."},
{"speiser_et_al__showed_that__ml_could_adequately_predict_day_by_day_outcome_in_patients_with_acetaminophen_induced_acute_liver_failure_alf": "The phrase \"showed that\" indicates that Speiser et al. demonstrated or provided evidence through their study. In this context, it means they presented data and results that support the effectiveness of machine learning in predicting day-by-day outcomes for patients with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure."},
{"ml_could_adequately_predict_day_by_day_outcome_in_patients_with_acetaminophen_induced_acute_liver_failure_alf": "Machine learning (ML) could adequately predict day-by-day outcomes in patients with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure (ALF) means that the machine learning models developed and tested by Speiser et al. were capable of accurately forecasting the daily progression and potential outcomes of patients suffering from this specific type of liver failure. This prediction was based on various data inputs and model algorithms, such as random forest extensions and variants, which were shown to have high accuracy in both training and validation datasets."},
{"the_authors": "The Authors refer to the researchers or authors of the proof-of-concept study conducted by Speiser et al. [18], who explored the use of machine learning (ML) models to predict outcomes in patients with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure (ALF)."},
{"the_authors__included__1042_patients_with_alf_between_1998_and_2016": "The predicate \"included\" signifies that the Authors incorporated or considered a specific group of patients in their study. This action is crucial for establishing the study's sample size and the population from which conclusions are drawn."},
{"1042_patients_with_alf_between_1998_and_2016": "The object \"1042 patients with ALF between 1998 and 2016\" describes the specific group of patients that the Authors included in their study. These patients suffered from acute liver failure (ALF) and were part of the study over an 18-year period, from 1998 to 2016."},
{"the_study": "The study refers to a proof-of-concept research conducted by Speiser et al., which aimed to predict day-by-day outcomes in patients with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure (ALF) using machine learning models. The study included a dataset of 1042 patients with ALF, divided into training and internal datasets, with the primary endpoint being the occurrence of encephalopathy within the first 7 days of hospitalization."},
{"the_study__showed_that__extensions_or_variants_of_random_forest_accurately_predicted_patient_outcomes": "The phrase \"showed that\" indicates the study's findings or results. In this context, it signifies that the study demonstrated the effectiveness of certain machine learning models in predicting patient outcomes."},
{"extensions_or_variants_of_random_forest_accurately_predicted_patient_outcomes": "Extensions or variants of random forest refers to modified or enhanced versions of the random forest algorithm, a popular machine learning technique used for classification and regression tasks. In this study, these modified random forest models, along with other machine learning algorithms like binary mixed model tree and support vector machine, were found to accurately predict patient outcomes in the context of acute liver failure."},
{"the_best_accuracy": "The best accuracy refers to the highest level of precision achieved in predicting outcomes for patients with acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure (ALF) using machine learning models. This metric is crucial for evaluating the effectiveness of different models in clinical settings.|Here is another description: The best accuracy refers to the highest level of predictive performance achieved in a study evaluating machine learning models for predicting outcomes in patients with acute liver failure (ALF)."},
{"the_best_accuracy__was_provided_by__the_binary_mixed_model_tree_in_the_training_dataset": "The phrase 'was provided by' indicates the source or method that yielded the highest accuracy in the study. It highlights the relationship between the accuracy achieved and the specific machine learning model used."},
{"the_binary_mixed_model_tree_in_the_training_dataset": "The binary mixed model tree in the training dataset is a specific machine learning model used in the study to predict patient outcomes. This model was found to provide the highest accuracy in the training phase, demonstrating its effectiveness in this context."},
{"the_best_accuracy__was_provided_by__binary_mixed_model_tree_and_support_vector_machine_in_the_validation_dataset": "The phrase 'was provided by' indicates the source or method that yielded the highest accuracy in the study. It highlights the specific model or combination of models that performed best in predicting patient outcomes."},
{"binary_mixed_model_tree_and_support_vector_machine_in_the_validation_dataset": "The binary mixed model tree and support vector machine in the validation dataset refers to the combination of two machine learning algorithms that together achieved the highest accuracy in predicting patient outcomes in the validation phase of the study. These models were found to be more effective than others tested, as indicated by their area under the curve (AUC) values."},
{"an_ann_based_model": "An ANN-based model refers to a machine learning model that utilizes artificial neural networks to process and analyze data. In this context, it was used to predict outcomes for patients with acute liver failure.|Here is another description: An ANN-based model, or Artificial Neural Network-based model, is a type of machine learning model inspired by the structure and function of the human brain. It is used for complex pattern recognition and prediction tasks, such as forecasting long-term outcomes in medical research."},
{"an_ann_based_model__was_applied_to__54_pediatric_patients_with_alf": "The phrase \"was applied to\" indicates that the ANN-based model was utilized or tested on a specific group of patients to evaluate its effectiveness in predicting outcomes."},
{"54_pediatric_patients_with_alf": "The 54 pediatric patients with ALF represent a specific subset of patients who were included in the study to assess the prognostic accuracy of the ANN-based model. These patients had acute liver failure and were under 18 years of age."},
{"the_ann_based_model": "The ANN-based model refers to an artificial neural network model applied in a study involving 54 pediatric patients with acute liver failure (ALF). This model was utilized to predict patient outcomes and was part of an effort to explore the efficacy of machine learning techniques in medical prognosis."},
{"the_ann_based_model__showed__a_good_prognostic_accuracy_when_compared_with_the_commonly_used_scores": "The predicate \"showed\" indicates that the ANN-based model demonstrated or exhibited a certain level of performance or result in the context of predicting outcomes for pediatric patients with ALF. It suggests that the model's performance was evaluated and compared against established metrics."},
{"a_good_prognostic_accuracy_when_compared_with_the_commonly_used_scores": "A good prognostic accuracy when compared with the commonly used scores refers to the performance metric of the ANN-based model, which was found to be effective in predicting patient outcomes. Specifically, the model's area under the curve (AUC) was 0.96, outperforming the MELD-PELD peak score (AUC: 0.86), although the study's small sample size and extended study period limited the robustness of the conclusions."},
{"the_study_period": "The study period refers to the duration over which the research or data collection for a particular study was conducted. In this context, it is the timeframe during which the study on pediatric patients with acute liver failure (ALF) was carried out."},
{"the_study_period__was__over_10_years": "The predicate \"was\" indicates the state or condition of the subject during the study. It serves as a link between the subject and the object, specifying that the study period had a certain duration."},
{"over_10_years": "The object \"over 10 years\" specifies the length of the study period. It indicates that the research spanned more than a decade, which might have implications for the robustness and generalizability of the study's conclusions due to potential changes in medical practices and patient populations over such a long duration."},
{"the_absence_of_external_validation": "The absence of external validation refers to the lack of independent verification of the results or models presented in studies, which is crucial for confirming their reliability and generalizability."},
{"the_absence_of_external_validation__may_represent__another_pitfall_in_the_interpretation_of_currently_published_results": "May represent indicates a potential or possible implication or consequence, suggesting that the absence of external validation could be interpreted as a significant issue or drawback."},
{"another_pitfall_in_the_interpretation_of_currently_published_results": "Another pitfall in the interpretation of currently published results refers to additional challenges or limitations that complicate the understanding or acceptance of the findings from studies, particularly in the context of machine learning applications in medical research."},
{"organ_allocation_systems": "Organ allocation systems are structured methodologies designed to distribute available organs to patients in need of transplants. These systems are crucial in managing the scarcity of organs and ensuring that they are used effectively to save lives.|Here is another description: Organ allocation systems are frameworks designed to distribute available organs for transplantation among patients in need. These systems are crucial in managing the limited supply of organs and ensuring that they are given to those who need them most."},
{"organ_allocation_systems__aim__to_maximize_the_use_of_available_organs": "The aim of organ allocation systems is to establish clear criteria and guidelines for how organs should be distributed among patients. This involves balancing various factors such as medical urgency, potential post-transplant survival, and overall transplant benefit to make equitable and effective decisions."},
{"to_maximize_the_use_of_available_organs": "Maximizing the use of available organs is a critical goal in organ allocation systems. This objective seeks to ensure that every organ is transplanted in a way that provides the greatest possible benefit to patients, reducing wait times and mortality rates on transplant waiting lists."},
{"organ_allocation_systems__aim__to_reduce_the_mortality_of_patients_on_the_waiting_list": "The aim of organ allocation systems is to establish a fair and efficient process for distributing organs. This involves considering various factors such as medical urgency, potential benefit, and overall utility to ensure that the allocation process is both equitable and effective in saving lives."},
{"to_reduce_the_mortality_of_patients_on_the_waiting_list": "Reducing the mortality of patients on the waiting list is a critical goal of organ allocation systems. This objective focuses on minimizing the number of deaths among patients awaiting transplantation by ensuring that organs are allocated to those who are most in need and have the highest likelihood of benefiting from the transplant."},
{"organ_allocation": "Organ allocation refers to the systematic process of determining which patients receive available organs for transplantation. This process is crucial in ensuring that organs are distributed in a manner that maximizes their utility and benefits the patients most in need.|Here is another description: Organ allocation refers to the systematic process of determining how organs for transplantation are distributed among patients on the waiting list. This process is crucial for ensuring that organs are used effectively to save lives and improve health outcomes.|Here is another description: Organ allocation refers to the systematic process of determining which patients receive available organs for transplantation. This process is crucial in ensuring that organs are distributed in a manner that maximizes their utility and benefits the recipients.|Here is another description: Organ allocation refers to the process of distributing available organs for transplantation to patients in need. This process involves prioritizing patients based on various factors including medical urgency, compatibility, and waiting time."},
{"organ_allocation__may_be_driven_by__urgency": "The phrase \"may be driven by\" indicates that the process of organ allocation can be influenced or guided by certain principles or factors. In this context, it suggests that the allocation of organs is not arbitrary but is instead based on specific criteria that are considered important in the decision-making process."},
{"urgency": "Urgency in the context of organ allocation refers to the principle of prioritizing patients who are estimated to have the shortest survival time without a transplant. This principle aims to address the immediate needs of patients who are at the highest risk of mortality while waiting for an organ, thereby ensuring that the most critical cases are addressed first.|Here is another description: Urgency in the context of organ allocation refers to a principle that prioritizes patients based on their immediate need for a transplant to survive."},
{"organ_allocation__may_be_driven_by__utility": "The phrase \"may be driven by\" indicates that the process of organ allocation can be influenced or guided by certain principles or criteria. In this context, it suggests that the decision-making in organ allocation can be based on different factors or objectives."},
{"utility": "Utility in the context of organ allocation refers to the principle of allocating organs to patients who are estimated to have the longest post-transplant survival. This approach aims to maximize the overall benefit and effectiveness of the transplantation process by ensuring that the organs are used in a way that provides the greatest long-term survival advantage to the recipients.|Here is another description: Utility in the context of organ allocation refers to a principle where organs are allocated to patients based on the estimated duration of their post-transplant survival. This approach aims to maximize the overall benefit derived from the transplantation process."},
{"organ_allocation__may_be_driven_by__transplant_benefit": "The phrase \"may be driven by\" indicates that the process of organ allocation can be influenced or guided by certain principles or factors. In this context, it suggests that the allocation of organs is not arbitrary but is based on specific criteria that aim to optimize patient outcomes."},
{"transplant_benefit": "Transplant benefit is a principle used in organ allocation that focuses on the difference in mean survival estimates between patients receiving a transplant and those who do not. This principle aims to allocate organs to patients who are expected to gain the most benefit from the transplant, thereby maximizing the overall effectiveness of the transplantation process.|Here is another description: Transplant benefit is a principle used in organ allocation systems to determine how organs should be distributed among patients. It focuses on the potential improvement in a patient's survival with a transplant compared to without one."},
{"urgency__definition__allocation_to_the_patient_estimated_to_have_the_shortest_survival_without_a_transplant": "The definition of urgency in organ allocation is characterized by the prioritization of patients who are estimated to have the shortest survival time without receiving a transplant."},
{"allocation_to_the_patient_estimated_to_have_the_shortest_survival_without_a_transplant": "Allocation to the patient estimated to have the shortest survival without a transplant means that the organ is given to the patient who, according to medical assessments, has the least time to live without the transplant, thus emphasizing the critical need for immediate intervention."},
{"utility__definition__allocation_to_the_patient_estimated_to_have_the_longest_post_transplant_survival": "The definition predicate here clarifies that utility, as a principle in organ allocation, specifically means prioritizing patients who are estimated to have the longest survival post-transplant. This definition helps in understanding the criteria used in the allocation process."},
{"allocation_to_the_patient_estimated_to_have_the_longest_post_transplant_survival": "Allocation to the patient estimated to have the longest post-transplant survival is a method within the utility principle of organ allocation. It involves selecting patients who are expected to live the longest after receiving a transplant, thereby optimizing the use of scarce organ resources."},
{"transplant_benefit__definition__allocation_based_on_the_difference_between_the_mean_survival_estimates_with_and_without_a_transplant": "The definition predicate here explains the specific meaning and application of the term \"transplant benefit\" within the context of organ allocation. It clarifies how this principle is operationalized in practice."},
{"allocation_based_on_the_difference_between_the_mean_survival_estimates_with_and_without_a_transplant": "Allocation based on the difference between the mean survival estimates with and without a transplant is a method that quantifies the potential benefit a patient might receive from undergoing a transplant. This approach aims to maximize the overall survival benefit for patients by considering both their expected survival with and without the transplant."},
{"a_unified_and_standardized_international_model": "A unified and standardized international model refers to a globally accepted and consistent framework or system that could be applied universally across different regions and cultures.|Here is another description: A unified and standardized international model refers to a globally accepted and consistent framework for a specific process or system, in this case, likely related to medical procedures or patient care protocols."},
{"a_unified_and_standardized_international_model__has_yet_to_be_adopted__due_to_significant_ethical_and_socio_cultural_differences_around_the_world": "The phrase \"has yet to be adopted\" indicates that despite efforts and discussions, this model has not been officially accepted or implemented on an international scale."},
{"due_to_significant_ethical_and_socio_cultural_differences_around_the_world": "The reason \"due to significant ethical and socio-cultural differences around the world\" highlights the challenges and barriers posed by varying moral principles, societal norms, and cultural practices that differ from one region to another, making it difficult to adopt a single, unified model globally."},
{"a_unified_and_standardized_international_model__has_yet_to_be_adopted__only_about_25_of_patients_undergo_benchmark_transplantations": "The phrase \"has yet to be adopted\" indicates that the subject, a unified and standardized international model, is still in a state of consideration or discussion and has not been officially implemented or accepted on a widespread scale."},
{"only_about_25_of_patients_undergo_benchmark_transplantations": "Only about 25% of patients undergo \u201cbenchmark\u201d transplantations suggests that a minority of patients receive a standard or optimal form of transplantation, indicating variability and inconsistency in the application of transplantation procedures across different centers or regions."},
{"a_model": "A model in this context refers to a system or framework used for liver transplantation (LT) allocation, specifically one that relies on biochemical indicators like the MELD score."},
{"a_model__may_be_less_appropriate__for_others": "The phrase \"may be less appropriate\" indicates that while the model could be effective or suitable for one group or population, it might not serve the same purpose or yield the same results for another group due to various factors such as ethical, socio-cultural differences, or variability in patient conditions."},
{"for_others": "\"For others\" in this context refers to different populations or groups of patients who may not benefit or be suited as well by the model due to the aforementioned differences and variabilities."},
{"most_lt_programs": "Most LT programs refer to the majority of liver transplantation programs that utilize an urgency-based allocation algorithm to determine the priority of patients for liver transplantation."},
{"most_lt_programs__rely_on__a_purely_biochemical_system_based_on_the_meld_score_or_subsequent_revisions": "The phrase'rely on' indicates a dependency or reliance of the subject (Most LT programs) on a specific system or method for making decisions or performing tasks, in this case, the allocation of livers for transplantation."},
{"a_purely_biochemical_system_based_on_the_meld_score_or_subsequent_revisions": "A purely biochemical system based on the MELD score, or subsequent revisions, refers to a method of assessing the severity of liver disease in patients awaiting liver transplantation. The MELD (Model for End-Stage Liver Disease) score is a numerical scale used to assess the severity of liver disease, and subsequent revisions may include updates or modifications to the original scoring system to improve its accuracy or applicability."},
{"meld_score__is_not_a_reliable_tool__in_utility_based_organ_allocation_systems": "The phrase \"is not a reliable tool\" indicates that the MELD score lacks the necessary accuracy or effectiveness in a specific context or application. In this case, it suggests that the MELD score does not adequately serve its intended purpose within utility-based organ allocation systems."},
{"in_utility_based_organ_allocation_systems": "Utility-based organ allocation systems are frameworks designed to distribute organs for transplantation in a manner that maximizes the overall benefit to patients. These systems aim to match donors and recipients in a way that optimizes post-transplant outcomes and resource utilization."},
{"meld_score__is_a_weak_predictor_of__post_lt_mortality": "Being a weak predictor of post-LT mortality means that the MELD score does not accurately forecast the likelihood of a patient's death after liver transplantation. Its predictive power is limited, making it less reliable for this specific outcome."},
{"post_lt_mortality": "Post-LT mortality refers to the death of a patient after undergoing liver transplantation. This outcome is critical in evaluating the success of the transplantation process and the effectiveness of pre-transplant assessment tools like the MELD score."},
{"appropriate_donor_recipient_pairing": "Appropriate donor\u2013recipient pairing refers to the careful matching of organ donors with recipients to ensure compatibility and maximize the success of the transplant. This process involves considering various factors such as blood type, tissue compatibility, and medical history to find the best possible match."},
{"appropriate_donor_recipient_pairing__is_important_in_order_to__improve_outcomes": "The phrase \"is important in order to\" signifies a critical relationship where the subject (appropriate donor\u2013recipient pairing) must be effectively managed to achieve a desired outcome. In this context, it highlights the necessity of precise matching in organ transplantation to enhance the likelihood of a successful procedure."},
{"improve_outcomes": "Improving outcomes in the context of organ transplantation means achieving better post-transplant results, such as increased graft survival, reduced complications, and enhanced overall patient health. This goal is crucial for ensuring the long-term success and quality of life for transplant recipients."},
{"several_scores": "Several scores refer to multiple scoring systems that have been created to evaluate and predict outcomes in organ transplantation, particularly liver transplantation. These scores are designed to assess various factors that influence the success of a transplant."},
{"several_scores__have_been_developed__considering_characteristics_of_donors_or_both_donors_and_recipients": "The phrase \"have been developed\" indicates that these scoring systems were created and refined over time through research and clinical practice. The development of these scores aims to improve the accuracy of predicting transplant outcomes and to facilitate better donor-recipient matching."},
{"considering_characteristics_of_donors_or_both_donors_and_recipients": "Considering characteristics of donors or both donors and recipients means that these scoring systems take into account various factors related to both the donor and the recipient. These characteristics may include medical history, organ quality, and other relevant variables that can impact the success of the transplant."},
{"posttransplant_outcome": "Posttransplant outcome refers to the results or consequences experienced by a patient after undergoing a transplant procedure. It encompasses various aspects such as graft survival, patient survival, and the occurrence of complications or adverse events."},
{"posttransplant_outcome__can_be_predicted_from__a_handful_of_variables": "The phrase \"can be predicted from\" indicates a relationship where the subject (posttransplant outcome) is influenced or determined by certain factors or variables. In this context, it suggests that the posttransplant outcome can be forecasted or estimated based on specific variables."},
{"a_handful_of_variables": "A handful of variables refers to a limited number of factors or parameters that are considered in predicting the posttransplant outcome. These variables may include characteristics of the donor, recipient, or surgical procedure, among others."},
{"briceno_et_al": "Briceno et al. refers to a research team led by Briceno that conducted a study on improving organ allocation methods. Their work focused on applying advanced statistical and computational techniques to enhance the prediction of post-transplant outcomes."},
{"briceno_et_al__applied__ml_to_the_complex_scenario_of_organ_allocation": "The predicate 'applied' indicates that Briceno et al. utilized or implemented a specific method or technology in their research. In this context, it means they employed machine learning techniques to address the challenges in organ allocation."},
{"ml_to_the_complex_scenario_of_organ_allocation": "Machine learning (ML) applied to the complex scenario of organ allocation involves using computational algorithms to analyze and predict outcomes based on a large set of variables related to donors, recipients, and surgical procedures. This approach aims to improve the accuracy of predicting graft survival and loss compared to traditional scoring systems."},
{"ann_model__combined__57_donor_recipient_or_surgery_related_variables": "The predicate \"combined\" indicates that the ANN model integrates and processes multiple variables to make its predictions. This combination of variables allows the model to provide more accurate predictions compared to traditional scoring systems like the MELD score."},
{"57_donor_recipient_or_surgery_related_variables": "The 57 donor-, recipient-, or surgery-related variables are a comprehensive set of factors considered by the ANN model to predict post-transplant outcomes. These variables include characteristics of both the donor and recipient, as well as surgical factors, which are crucial for determining the success of a liver transplant."},
{"ann_model__was_more_accurate_than__commonly_used_logistic_regression_models": "The phrase \"was more accurate than\" indicates a comparative evaluation of the ANN model's predictive performance against other models, specifically highlighting its superior accuracy in forecasting outcomes."},
{"commonly_used_logistic_regression_models": "Commonly used logistic regression models are statistical methods employed to predict binary outcomes based on one or more predictor variables. In the context of organ transplantation, these models are often used to estimate the risk of graft survival or loss."},
{"ann_model__predicted__3_month_graft_survival_ann_vs_meld_auc_0_80_vs_0_50_p_0_001": "The predicate 'predicted' indicates that the ANN model was used to forecast a specific outcome, in this case, the 3-month graft survival after liver transplantation. The model's predictions were compared against the MELD score to evaluate its accuracy."},
{"3_month_graft_survival_ann_vs_meld_auc_0_80_vs_0_50_p_0_001": "The 3-month graft survival is a critical outcome measure in liver transplantation, indicating whether the transplanted liver remains functional three months post-surgery. The ANN model showed a significantly higher accuracy (AUC: 0.80) compared to the MELD score (AUC: 0.50) in predicting this outcome, with a p-value of 0.001 indicating statistical significance."},
{"ann_model__predicted__graft_loss_ann_vs_meld_auc_0_82_vs_0_41_p_0_001": "The predicate \"predicted\" indicates that the ANN model was used to forecast or estimate the likelihood of a specific outcome, in this case, graft loss, by processing and analyzing the input data."},
{"graft_loss_ann_vs_meld_auc_0_82_vs_0_41_p_0_001": "Graft loss refers to the failure of a transplanted organ to function properly post-transplantation. In this context, it is the outcome that the ANN model predicted with higher accuracy (AUC: 0.82) compared to the MELD score (AUC: 0.41), as indicated by the statistical significance (P = 0.001)."},
{"ann_model__was_confirmed_more_accurate_than__above_mentioned_scores_28_29": "The predicate \"was confirmed more accurate than\" indicates that the ANN model's predictive performance was evaluated and found to be superior to other scoring systems in terms of accuracy, as measured by the area under the curve (AUC) and statistical significance (P-value)."},
{"above_mentioned_scores_28_29": "The \"above-mentioned scores [28,29]\" refer to other scoring systems that were previously discussed in the context, which consider the characteristics of donors or both donors and recipients to predict post-transplant outcomes. These scores were found to be less accurate than the ANN model in predicting 3-month graft survival and graft loss."},
{"ann_model__was_further_validated_in__an_english_cohort_of_858_patients_who_underwent_lt_between_2002_and_2010": "The phrase \"was further validated in\" indicates that the ANN model underwent additional testing and verification in a specific cohort to confirm its accuracy and reliability in predicting outcomes."},
{"an_english_cohort_of_858_patients_who_underwent_lt_between_2002_and_2010": "The English cohort of 858 patients who underwent liver transplantation (LT) between 2002 and 2010 is a group of patients used to further validate the ANN model. Patients with hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) were excluded from this cohort."},
{"ann_model__predicted__3_and_12_month_graft_survival_ann_auc_0_94_and_0_78_respectively": "The predicate \"predicted\" indicates that the ANN model was used to forecast specific outcomes, in this case, the survival rates of liver grafts at 3 and 12 months post-transplantation. The model's predictions are quantified using the Area Under the Curve (AUC) metric, which measures the model's ability to distinguish between different outcomes."},
{"3_and_12_month_graft_survival_ann_auc_0_94_and_0_78_respectively": "The object \"3- and 12-month graft survival (ANN AUC: 0.94 and 0.78, respectively)\" refers to the specific outcomes that the ANN model predicted. These outcomes are the survival rates of liver grafts at 3 and 12 months after transplantation. The AUC values of 0.94 and 0.78 indicate the model's high accuracy in predicting these survival rates, outperforming other commonly used scoring systems."},
{"ann_model__predicted__graft_loss_ann_auc_0_94_and_0_82_respectively": "The predicate \"predicted\" indicates that the ANN model was used to forecast or estimate the likelihood of a specific outcome, in this case, graft loss, based on the input variables it was trained on."},
{"graft_loss_ann_auc_0_94_and_0_82_respectively": "Graft loss refers to the failure of a transplanted organ to function properly post-transplantation. In the context of the ANN model, graft loss is the outcome that the model predicted with AUC values of 0.94 and 0.82 for 3- and 12-month periods, respectively, showcasing the model's accuracy in forecasting this critical post-transplantation event."},
{"ann_model__performed_better_than__in_the_training_cohort": "The ANN model performed better than other commonly used models and scores in predicting 3-month and 12-month graft survival and graft loss in both the training and validation cohorts."},
{"in_the_training_cohort": "The training cohort refers to the initial group of 1003 patients who received a liver transplant in Spain between 2007 and 2008, used to develop and train the ANN model. The model's performance in this cohort served as a baseline for comparison with other models and scores."},
