{"most_accurate_model": "The most accurate model refers to the ANN-based model developed by Khosravi et al. for predicting long-term outcomes after liver transplantation. This model was found to be the most effective among the various models tested."},
{"most_accurate_model__included__16_of_37_baseline_predictors": "The term \"included\" in this context signifies that the most accurate model incorporated a specific subset of predictors from a larger set available for analysis. These predictors were deemed essential for the model's accuracy and predictive power."},
{"16_of_37_baseline_predictors": "The 16 of 37 baseline predictors represent a carefully selected subset of variables used in the most accurate model. These predictors include five recipient characteristics, 10 intra or postoperative variables, and chronic rejection, which were considered critical for predicting long-term outcomes after liver transplantation.|Here is another description: The subject refers to a subset of 16 predictors out of a total of 37 baseline predictors that were considered in a study for predicting long-term outcomes after liver transplantation.|Here is another description: The subject refers to a selection of 16 predictors out of a total of 37 baseline predictors used in a study. These predictors are part of an ANN-based model designed to predict long-term outcomes after liver transplantation.|Here is another description: The subject refers to a selection of 16 out of 37 baseline predictors used in a study. These predictors encompass various characteristics and variables related to liver transplant patients."},
